#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305d1aaf-bbea-5c5f-a549-96aeb913edda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279014} {:answer/id #uuid "7052a4ed-9911-5fd7-bc04-95f5aa7e4565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610} {:answer/id #uuid "774d71e4-5b4e-524f-b577-edc0dd3ee10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279657} {:answer/id #uuid "ca898aae-6598-59af-88de-b8842924f741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280367} {:answer/id #uuid "cd52ceb4-0135-536f-8588-e294bafcb89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd52ceb4-0135-536f-8588-e294bafcb89b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280401} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6048b0a-8782-5f08-a33d-6088d1e77789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b54a2292-f496-593a-8f3d-c0762d77f082" :question/math-academy-id "q-162606" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134368 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07df9b30-7494-5a29-bbf7-545bfb801b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324043} {:answer/id #uuid "43c82af1-e249-5406-8916-4edf2249835d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy\\,i+8xy\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324517} {:answer/id #uuid "46fbffe9-1708-536c-9450-fbf68226d2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324544} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0d70f3-b9ef-52d9-833f-60c57b0ef2ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324594} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed01203-bd0d-5479-858a-f2f7671e56d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07df9b30-7494-5a29-bbf7-545bfb801b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324043} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a071631-1415-51cd-b3f7-53b385114597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8381fb3-8431-5be5-b51b-a5d43ddf8c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-41817" :question/problem "Given that $F=2x{y}^{2}\\,i+4{x}^{2}y\\,j,$ what is $\\text{div}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =2{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f95316f-165c-5113-8ee9-c9367379b55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324115} {:answer/id #uuid "23c7a9c5-c903-5481-b43f-909fffa5ca4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324265} {:answer/id #uuid "ae04b775-dac5-5210-bd8d-4c5a06b587a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325444} {:answer/id #uuid "bae12623-0161-5a6a-b882-9ee3f846c29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325578} {:answer/id #uuid "ce705a3e-a8b9-5748-843b-3a1311fd41b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce705a3e-a8b9-5748-843b-3a1311fd41b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325765} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4fb8b77-4723-5632-b763-838f5f897e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17dfa481-85bd-5863-b436-260f29cb7515" :question/math-academy-id "q-41839" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=xyz\\,i+{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,j+({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz)\\,k.$ \nEvaluate $∇\\cdot F$ at the point $(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{-y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi z+2xz) \\\\  & =yz-{e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x+\\pi {e}^{-y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi z+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate $\\text{div}\\,F$ at the point $(1,0,0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(∇\\cdot F)(1,0,0) & =(0)(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi {e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(1) \\\\  & =0-(-1)+\\pi (1)+2 \\\\  & =1+\\pi +2 \\\\  & =\\pi +3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d95192b-f90c-5222-9284-d5964b68929b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132260} {:answer/id #uuid "9811580a-9a4c-5cae-b93f-8fd017dafbe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-yi - zj - xk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132261} {:answer/id #uuid "09fbced1-93c8-576e-a89e-8374e5fcba7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "yi + zj + xk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132262} {:answer/id #uuid "566a2e25-a40d-56a1-94ed-4593f8fbe01c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-yi + zj - xk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132263} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7af965-ca09-5326-8611-2d1a13330464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "yi - zj + xk" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132264}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7886041-1fa1-565d-a7c5-c176ca25c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132259}] :question/id #uuid "c3a2ad27-232a-567b-825a-c40b631d791e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41883" :question/problem "Given that $F(x, y, z) = xyi + yzj + xzk$, what is $curlF$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a235db-fe30-5523-a6f2-1e7015a51178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i - πj + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132266} {:answer/id #uuid "a24521cf-55f3-5015-939b-a0cc82725ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132267} {:answer/id #uuid "199823a5-e425-55bd-ac92-cb08df6ff59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i - πj" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132268} {:answer/id #uuid "26bbb2ee-97a5-5ba6-a39c-bf211651dd8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- πj + k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132269} {:answer/id #uuid "6955754e-91e9-5e29-a742-fb32e4672dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132270}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d59174-8d79-514e-8de5-8030757c35f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132265}] :question/id #uuid "edc90e26-2ff6-5c4d-97cc-a6c6e64cf17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41899" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F = x\\cos yzi + y\\ln xzk$. Evaluate $∇ \\cdot F$ at the point $(1, \\frac{π}{2}, 1)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f72d1468-b59f-55f3-9d92-ba00ddb0db88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132296} {:answer/id #uuid "fa256ecb-1155-587e-a827-4c7f7584f6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132297} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5293a6-7b3b-5ed3-a5f8-13eb0ad2c78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132298} {:answer/id #uuid "5d10c8c0-0ad7-587d-9d4f-df30df144733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132299} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3d7569-0df8-52ec-b06f-90609c9c6e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132300}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bb773cb5-403e-5d2c-95a1-5dbb8eba2e0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8b5adde-1913-5b7b-8715-f70ae223e349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370941} {:answer/id #uuid "5f438494-28b7-57fb-80d6-a3fe7e73a368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370942} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b51f75-a136-57d4-bf07-dc90a22f9315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370943} {:answer/id #uuid "2260ecea-4586-5fe7-ac66-3cc46eb39c1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370944} {:answer/id #uuid "2f828455-578e-54d4-843d-b9476bb1f31f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b51f75-a136-57d4-bf07-dc90a22f9315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370943} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f079002-de78-5504-85d4-0c00298a5bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c3df1fb-6ba7-5d92-9c02-3f4fe719a847" :question/math-academy-id "q-42215" :question/problem "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997d82a-e8fd-5876-bff6-f2a494d2c16e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510841} {:answer/id #uuid "2e996338-8bd4-5f85-acba-798d27184bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510881} {:answer/id #uuid "91c650e3-c5a3-551b-acac-5cc206d90d87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511082} {:answer/id #uuid "a50bd914-811d-5009-9a3f-16c3d9e26a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511129} {:answer/id #uuid "e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5105f35-40d7-5290-bc6b-8489218cf6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511263} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f782c02-0bd7-576d-a703-443eb8f872fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "097af826-94c0-5b59-ad65-7d59555aa9e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-42221" :question/problem "![](images/44/4408d1fcee1d168c6ab21ea53cf24845093bad9cc608299a36f0510c1636cc03.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9daf741d-ed1d-5418-9a08-c962cd04f234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392496} {:answer/id #uuid "29dfbde4-ae70-5670-a1c3-f06b16942b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392497} {:answer/id #uuid "3efa1f4b-972e-5984-9ba9-47ae745edcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392498} {:answer/id #uuid "607a8eb7-ea64-55b5-903c-5325b01df2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392499} {:answer/id #uuid "de2df677-04a7-58a1-b729-58fb2faf6e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de2df677-04a7-58a1-b729-58fb2faf6e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392500} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba482bd4-ad69-5775-80d8-0a8566f264c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "321757db-d917-5b82-a83a-32bb4ce151f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42238" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f51a25843259881994e3dca0781b780878bec349930b76fbf449dc824e69ec2.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f48c0b-2a3d-5163-b8e8-0c7233eb05fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132356} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d132b-6218-5a49-8b31-e23dbbbfbe93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132357} {:answer/id #uuid "ed54db10-308f-5357-92ca-c739526b9c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132358} {:answer/id #uuid "fab5eb83-c4a0-5d86-82d7-ebea1c4f60b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132359} {:answer/id #uuid "6d37aff8-08ac-5983-b3ea-04ddf202a770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f48c0b-2a3d-5163-b8e8-0c7233eb05fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132356} :answer-field/id #uuid "30db4754-f10f-5c4e-a207-166a2bda9bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c299dba6-b9c4-559b-b05a-803d3465ef0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42298" :question/problem "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4t\\,i-3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{t}^{3})\\cdot 4t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151947 :question/id #uuid "a18b37e2-4ab1-5fd1-b985-3021172abc41" :question/math-academy-id "e-6306" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2} \\, \\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y = x$ for $\\, 0 \\le x \\le 4.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = t.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 4\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^3}{x^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{3}}{{t}^{2}}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}({4}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151948 :question/id #uuid "b4f26e02-fb0f-5577-90f4-52e7eeabd857" :question/math-academy-id "e-6310" :question/problem "Consider the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{y^2}{x} \\,\\textrm ds,\n$$\n\n where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\dfrac{x^3}3$ between the points $(0,0)$ and $(3,9).$\nWrite the line integral in the form \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y = y(t) = \\dfrac{t^3}{3}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{t^3}3 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le 3,\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\right)j \\\\  & =i+{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y^2}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}{t}=\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{5}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a322310f-e99f-5b58-bbe2-114f6837ba94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150870} {:answer/id #uuid "b8209043-5a80-5226-82bd-6f75cf33406d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150871} {:answer/id #uuid "da28f5eb-984c-5b86-a689-fbfeb01d1d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{t^{4}}{4}}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150872} {:answer/id #uuid "56bf868b-f472-569b-8d26-4afb16730910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150873} {:answer/id #uuid "56e02937-08a7-5e79-a865-33a9bfc788fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{t^{2}}{2}}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150874}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b137a932-595c-5ba6-b009-cece172e6237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150869}] :question/id #uuid "865867f1-c4a6-5f27-b57d-d7e0f05be1da" :question/math-academy-id "q-76188" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)\\frac{y}{x}ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y = \\frac{x^{2}}{2}$ between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(2, 2)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b69fbe-6862-5988-9187-0cfdeadb5248" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{e}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342385} {:answer/id #uuid "96eb1843-e485-5232-b8f1-b3464dbf007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343559} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e3275a-4203-5060-8151-e07c74483034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{e}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343840} {:answer/id #uuid "ce400b51-078a-5d17-a117-a5b67c6be11b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{e}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{t+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344055} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6e87ee-47c0-5abb-b9bf-b56fbbc45a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{e}t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96eb1843-e485-5232-b8f1-b3464dbf007b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343559} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8730e8b-8a1d-5469-b535-2eaa510779c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38412a6f-7fd7-5b03-94a1-fc852b38a0b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76189" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5y{e}^{-x}\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y={e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le e?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=5y{e}^{-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{e}5\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f6daa38-7471-5f48-a645-2a58b0ccbb8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342894} {:answer/id #uuid "4f78e0c3-9035-5dc2-8ab5-f198ada876f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342898} {:answer/id #uuid "57ea0380-1042-5b37-9a3a-4b0d0e5dc158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342971} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d08ff9-8579-514b-818e-d1904f4ed1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343798} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c6af53-8fe9-5d44-852a-2dfdb428e5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c6af53-8fe9-5d44-852a-2dfdb428e5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344257} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a857e8-a364-5f7f-9a5f-59f962b7904c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78a90830-8528-5d8e-9b99-56c7720a064a" :question/math-academy-id "q-76483" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$ along the curve $y={e}^{x},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)={e}^{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})j \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2({e}^{t})^{2}=2{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{2t}$, $\\text{d}u=2{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{2t}\\,\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({10}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(10\\sqrt{10}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}(5\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c03a658-3ced-5c53-8935-8cd4c7daaee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150984} {:answer/id #uuid "92aaba17-f7e1-5c37-9c23-985c2dd63e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150985} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5aa0ec-6082-55e3-9533-1691c19eb313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150986} {:answer/id #uuid "1df132c5-ce6b-50c6-af6a-10e7041781c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150987} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1e765a-764a-5dc0-a4c7-3a07dea17ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150988}] :answer-field/id #uuid "686dc23d-0f1e-5e4d-9da1-a23da84b778b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150983}] :question/id #uuid "cc7e2b92-479b-55b5-8bf7-4a42b47fa43c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76508" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = x(y - 1)$ along the line $y = 2x + 1$, $0 \\le x \\le 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102 :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e47cfcf3da66707e6bc898d099868bfbd6d5163dcd38d47e4c79b2180e89ec6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103 :question/id #uuid "859e1d71-21cb-5e30-9d60-a4b21358fe1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7410" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa730804a15decac8450255e714ca1e0697f4f2326eb615d4d035a2870a49dce.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $$\n\\displaystyle x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104 :question/id #uuid "ac36f233-1f32-5cba-a35e-aa8e48207a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-7411" :question/problem "![](images/94/940f547f886f1b680cbb37ef950438e6a4f33bd3c84df28a5e00480631c97711.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5599a1-f278-5f47-99a3-11e721c640de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275763} {:answer/id #uuid "304759b0-aaf7-59c1-9090-4a85c6aa15b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275907} {:answer/id #uuid "49f4a40a-2c87-57e8-be51-2ef3a5b54f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276130} {:answer/id #uuid "8eeca947-3eeb-5410-b5cd-33e1f5246ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276773} {:answer/id #uuid "f0c0486b-2790-5e6a-bbc4-42e3e621f5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0c0486b-2790-5e6a-bbc4-42e3e621f5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277591} :answer-field/id #uuid "64150f3e-bcf2-5937-bf2c-ae0c28fb7370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2616ffa6-4ad5-55b3-bbe1-ee00aff863bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161102" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0e8a196dc27f333db4179b5c318ccd3aa4b385c0752c570fbfedc0226b3b15b.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63ff2307-bdea-53b3-88e5-97b96d5ad36a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489749} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4cd776-9de6-538f-9d94-7a3efcb04fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489772} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4aa5b4-4bf8-52ba-bb0c-de13688b61cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489774} {:answer/id #uuid "c761b339-ab83-5bb8-80db-4cc0d6bebede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489946} {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce7b078-805e-5d09-a2f3-9ad87e8072c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489969} :answer-field/id #uuid "d25f125e-94ad-5225-b5e5-f24049a7a3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d182d5c-4098-5732-b594-84e4e6e5bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83789" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b19c89df-368c-5ff4-a99f-2130d93e9dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}(r - r^{2})drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155950} {:answer/id #uuid "d0956b7f-1504-5111-aa2d-dadaea419910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}(1 - r)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155951} {:answer/id #uuid "f94726e4-f834-507c-bd0f-126953908b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}(r - r^{2})drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155952} {:answer/id #uuid "05195b1f-39dd-5a34-a61e-bb83cc4cb37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}(1 - r^{2})drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155953} {:answer/id #uuid "21e7a4ec-b548-52d4-a55d-4e5504478dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}r(1 - r^{2})drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155954}] :answer-field/id #uuid "03eb7787-d58a-530f-9eb4-803ccae5c506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155949}] :question/id #uuid "40cd448b-69a6-51cf-b9ad-88a39b83e9d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83948" :question/problem "![](images/94/94bc62a00cf36b5d54eed30b9277ce268b5bdbd6d5c76c0d9fb7764e0083a2d0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the plane is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3$, as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∬_(D)(1 - x^{2} - y^{2})dA\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c7c83-2673-55cc-b80f-82dd032a52b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394561} {:answer/id #uuid "781c89a4-4fc2-5fdd-81c9-1fe0439559bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394562} {:answer/id #uuid "c04067e2-1051-59f7-ba78-7d685ba5c6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394563} {:answer/id #uuid "7e63b9da-492f-5a21-ae92-0344b2806a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a268e5-f9a7-5175-82f6-429e900d7180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394560} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e89d210-e453-5f03-84ad-9efe39e4cea9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83980" :question/problem "![](images/92/928fb72015b78436f95895799c2846d1a0b34242b7216d31a6ed6bdda46728a1.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and the line $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For $x=2\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the point $(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2}).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/37/375e270ce444d9f62b50f9a25abfae781113fdad895833d693061ffffe93f5d3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{4}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939507be-1c81-5ae7-8ee3-1c8b4987b74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0de5db-9466-5fa3-9728-3739822adce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155966} {:answer/id #uuid "339ef220-3e41-588b-8376-adbd3ff3fd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489630} {:answer/id #uuid "68754c06-e278-58cb-aad9-4cc8f6c4502c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489765} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f50510-55ec-59ec-bc2f-bcefacaba339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "339ef220-3e41-588b-8376-adbd3ff3fd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489630} :answer-field/id #uuid "801f0ec1-f516-55ae-acb8-d095f0095e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "932bfdfe-cb1f-5acf-bcb0-ab10a83a0361" :question/math-academy-id "q-83986" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfaa18cb3295d3bb1641f440a3c1642758fe793c3605cf7d010121a68bbff47d.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/46/4660d61851814c87afc7c140dd87e1354785f55cafe4fbc911fd369f768914e3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{4-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}r\\sqrt{4-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{[-\\frac{1}{3}(4-{r}^{2})^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-3\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcb979f-3921-508f-9b2b-ae8e7145b9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r(\\cos θ - \\sin θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156010} {:answer/id #uuid "0eef47cb-4788-507e-b1f4-c3795d6ae85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}r^{2}(\\cos θ - \\sin θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156011} {:answer/id #uuid "2033d184-d234-5f04-a79e-49eafcb830da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}r^{2}(\\sin θ - \\cos θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156012} {:answer/id #uuid "2274961c-375d-58ac-94ad-7773f6f452f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}r^{3}(\\cos θ - \\sin θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156013} {:answer/id #uuid "751a9151-32fb-5553-b22a-f8cb9eecd5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4}r(\\cos θ - \\sin θ)drdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156014}] :answer-field/id #uuid "94ce5ea4-7b87-505a-8ba6-b0fd5e7fa28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025d785b-02fc-5324-908f-2d5dd38c73d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394649} {:answer/id #uuid "01d18614-93e9-5aaa-b7e4-447dd17b7f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394650} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d5b1d0-1acd-5eb9-b07e-5ba4ecc1f5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394651} {:answer/id #uuid "a25e74a8-0bba-52c9-a7d3-ce68080ff54a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{r}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394652} {:answer/id #uuid "05c07a46-a840-5836-8a95-6a34b4f0f4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01d18614-93e9-5aaa-b7e4-447dd17b7f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394650} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4bfa259-3228-56fb-ad70-431761ce87bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97fc9bc2-d10b-5e4f-be32-39965512311e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85059" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f7f37b10d483f7ce2bf147f316195b4be578ae62da8538a3aac4a8dbfa2ec1c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}r(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166696 :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166716 :question/id #uuid "3f266f66-1d79-5864-b497-1bc1b138c318" :question/math-academy-id "e-9499" :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf F = (e^{y^2} - 2xy) \\, \\mathbf i + (2xye^{y^2} - y) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf G = (3x^2 - y^2) \\, \\mathbf i + (4 - 2xy) \\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf H = (xy^2 + 1) \\, \\mathbf i + 2xy \\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ we have $P = e^{y^2} - 2xy$ and $Q = 2xye^{y^2} - y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2ye^{y^2} - 2x, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2ye^{y^2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf G,$ we have $P = 3x^2 - y^2$ and $Q = 4 - 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = -2y, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = -2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2,$ we conclude that $\\mathbf{G}$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- For the vector field $\\mathbf H,$ we have $P = xy^2 + 1$ and $Q = 2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $\\mathbf{H}$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
